📮 TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) — "The Reliable Overthinker"

TCP is that friend who triple-checks everything before moving forward.

✅ Reliable — Every packet MUST arrive. If one gets lost, it's resent until confirmed.
✅ Ordered — Packets arrive in the EXACT order they were sent (even if they take different routes).
✅ Connection-based — Does a "handshake" first (like saying "hi, you there? cool, let's talk" before sending anything) 🤝

How it guarantees delivery:

"Hey I sent packet 1... did you get it?" ✅ "Yes!" → "Cool, sending packet 2 now..."

It's SLOW-ish because of all this checking, but nothing gets lost or scrambled.

Used for: Things where losing even 1 byte = disaster

🌐 Loading websites (HTTP/HTTPS)
📧 Emails
📁 File downloads
💳 Banking transactions

(Imagine your bank transfer losing a packet with your money amount in it 💀 — TCP prevents that nightmare)

📡 UDP (User Datagram Protocol) — "The Chill, No-Filter Friend"

UDP is that friend who just yeets the message and doesn't care if you got it or not.

❌ No reliability — Sends packets and doesn't check if they arrived. Lost packet? Doesn't care, moves on.
❌ No ordering — Packets might arrive out of order, and UDP won't fix that.
❌ No handshake — No "hi, you there?" — just straight up sends data immediately. Faster start.

How it "guarantees" delivery:

"Here's data. Bye." 🏃💨 (no confirmation needed)

It's FAST because there's zero overhead/checking.

Used for: Things where speed > perfection, and old data is USELESS anyway

🎥 Video calls (Zoom, Google Meet)
🎮 Online gaming
📞 Voice calls (VoIP)
📺 Live streaming